# Copy to .env if you need it. Docker Compose reads this file automatically.
#
# You probably do not need it. The image carries a working configuration, both
# long-lived keys are generated into the data volume on first boot, and the
# administrator account, channels and API keys are all created at
# http://localhost:18080/admin.
#
# This file is for the two cases that are not the default:
#
#   1. You want the host ports to be something other than 18080 and 2525.
#   2. You mount your own configuration (see the volume in docker-compose.yml)
#      and it references these names with `!env`. A name that is referenced and
#      missing makes the service refuse to start, which is deliberate: an empty
#      credential is worse than a loud failure, because it looks like it worked.
#
# .env is in .gitignore and .dockerignore. Keep it that way.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- ports

# The port on the host. The service listens on 8080 inside the container
# either way; only the mapping changes. Set this when 18080 is already taken.
NOTIFYRELAY_HTTP_PORT=18080

# Likewise for SMTP inbound, which is off unless a configuration enables it.
NOTIFYRELAY_SMTP_PORT=2525

# ------------------------------------------------------- keys (optional)

# Both are generated into <data>/keys/ on first boot and read from there after,
# so leave these blank unless you are restoring a deployment whose keys you
# already have, or you keep secrets in a manager and would rather they never
# touch the data volume.
#
# NOTIFYRELAY_SECRET_KEY seals channel credentials in the database. Losing it
# means losing every credential sealed with it, and no backup of the database
# alone can recover them.
#
# NOTIFYRELAY_SESSION_KEY signs operator sessions. It must be a different value
# from NOTIFYRELAY_SECRET_KEY: one key serving two purposes means a weakness in
# the weaker use is a weakness in both.
#
# Generate either with:
#
#   docker compose run --rm notifyrelay --gen-key
#
# NOTIFYRELAY_SECRET_KEY=
# NOTIFYRELAY_SESSION_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- channels
#
# Only needed when a mounted configuration references them. The default
# configuration does not: channels are configured in the operator UI, and their
# credentials are stored sealed in the database.
#
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# WECOM_CORP_SECRET=
# DINGTALK_SECRET=
# FEISHU_SECRET=
# SLACK_TOKEN=
# WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------- operator account
#
# Nothing to set. The first person to open /admin creates the administrator,
# and that page closes permanently afterwards. To put the account in the
# configuration file instead — for a deployment that must not depend on whoever
# reaches the port first — generate a hash and mount a configuration:
#
#   docker compose run --rm notifyrelay --hash-password 'your-password'
#
# and set admin.username and admin.password_hash in it.
